Randy Jackson (raj@dduck.ctt.bellcore.com) made a number of interesting
suggestions and then Ari Huttunen (ahuttune@niksula.hut.fi) commented on
them.  

>>1) A new object called a compass. When applied the compass would
>>   point in a direction ("Your compass points SE"), which would
>>   be the direction of the down stairs in the dungeon levels and
>I don't think this would be particularly useful. Hack levels are so
>small, that you find the stairs quite quickly by trial and error.

It might be more interesting to have the compass point in the
direction of goal or magic, and "spin wildly" when the level is
cleaned out.

>Please, don't put rifles or hand guns or grenades or similar 'modern' 
>inventions to hack. They just SPOIL THE ATMOSPHERE. 

Like stethoscopes, ice boxes, and flash cameras?  Hack needn't be 
restricted to some fourth-grade D&D view of medieval times.

>In 2.3 grenades were far too powerful, but something
>less powerful would be nice; implement potions of oil. When you lit 'em
>and throw 'em, they work like grenades. And cause fire damage.

This always cracks me up.  Light a potion of oil?  They work like
grenades?  Not in this reality.

>>4) Wand of petrification - This would turn doors into walls and corridors
